  n<//-------------------------------------------------------> u<340> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<341> f<1> l<209:1> el<209:58>
  n<//    DBG_UART -> Enable UART (8N1) debug interface> u<341> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<342> f<1> l<210:1> el<210:52>
  n<//    DBG_I2C  -> Enable I2C debug interface> u<342> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<343> f<1> l<211:1> el<211:45>
  n<//-------------------------------------------------------> u<343> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<344> f<1> l<212:1> el<212:58>
  n<//`define DBG_I2C> u<344> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<345> f<1> l<214:1> el<214:18>
  n<//-------------------------------------------------------> u<345> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<346> f<1> l<217:1> el<217:58>
  n<// Enable the I2C broadcast address> u<346> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<347> f<1> l<218:1> el<218:36>
  n<//-------------------------------------------------------> u<347> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<348> f<1> l<219:1> el<219:58>
  n<// For multicore systems, a common I2C broadcast address> u<348> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<349> f<1> l<220:1> el<220:57>
  n<// can be given to all oMSP cores in order to> u<349> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<350> f<1> l<221:1> el<221:46>
  n<// synchronously RESET, START, STOP, or STEP all CPUs> u<350> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<351> f<1> l<222:1> el<222:54>
  n<// at once with a single I2C command.> u<351> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<352> f<1> l<223:1> el<223:38>
  n<// If you have a single openMSP430 in your system,> u<352> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<353> f<1> l<224:1> el<224:51>
  n<// this option can stay commented-out.> u<353> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<354> f<1> l<225:1> el<225:39>
  n<//-------------------------------------------------------> u<354> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<355> f<1> l<226:1> el<226:58>
  n<//`define DBG_I2C_BROADCAST> u<355> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<356> f<1> l<227:1> el<227:28>
  n<//-------------------------------------------------------> u<356> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<357> f<1> l<230:1> el<230:58>
  n<// Number of hardware breakpoint/watchpoint units> u<357> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<358> f<1> l<231:1> el<231:50>
  n<// (each unit contains two hardware addresses available> u<358> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<359> f<1> l<232:1> el<232:56>
  n<// for breakpoints or watchpoints):> u<359> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<360> f<1> l<233:1> el<233:36>
  n<//   - DBG_HWBRK_0 -> Include hardware breakpoints unit 0> u<360> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<361> f<1> l<234:1> el<234:58>
  n<//   - DBG_HWBRK_1 -> Include hardware breakpoints unit 1> u<361> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<362> f<1> l<235:1> el<235:58>
  n<//   - DBG_HWBRK_2 -> Include hardware breakpoints unit 2> u<362> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<363> f<1> l<236:1> el<236:58>
  n<//   - DBG_HWBRK_3 -> Include hardware breakpoints unit 3> u<363> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<364> f<1> l<237:1> el<237:58>
  n<//-------------------------------------------------------> u<364> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<365> f<1> l<238:1> el<238:58>
  n<// Please keep in mind that hardware breakpoints only> u<365> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<366> f<1> l<239:1> el<239:54>
  n<// make sense whenever the program memory is not an SRAM> u<366> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<367> f<1> l<240:1> el<240:57>
  n<// (i.e. Flash/OTP/ROM/...) or when you are interested> u<367> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<368> f<1> l<241:1> el<241:55>
  n<// in data breakpoints.> u<368> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<369> f<1> l<242:1> el<242:24>
  n<//-------------------------------------------------------> u<369> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<370> f<1> l<243:1> el<243:58>
  n<//`define  DBG_HWBRK_0> u<370> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<371> f<1> l<244:1> el<244:23>
  n<//`define  DBG_HWBRK_1> u<371> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<372> f<1> l<245:1> el<245:23>
  n<//`define  DBG_HWBRK_2> u<372> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<373> f<1> l<246:1> el<246:23>
  n<//`define  DBG_HWBRK_3> u<373> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<374> f<1> l<247:1> el<247:23>
  n<//-------------------------------------------------------> u<374> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<375> f<1> l<250:1> el<250:58>
  n<// Enable/Disable the hardware breakpoint RANGE mode> u<375> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<376> f<1> l<251:1> el<251:53>
  n<//-------------------------------------------------------> u<376> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<377> f<1> l<252:1> el<252:58>
  n<// When enabled this feature allows the hardware breakpoint> u<377> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<378> f<1> l<253:1> el<253:60>
  n<// units to stop the cpu whenever an instruction or data> u<378> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<379> f<1> l<254:1> el<254:57>
  n<// access lays within an address range.> u<379> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<380> f<1> l<255:1> el<255:40>
  n<// Note that this feature is not supported by GDB.> u<380> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<381> f<1> l<256:1> el<256:51>
  n<//-------------------------------------------------------> u<381> t<LINE_COMMENT> p<10007> s<382> f<1> l<257:1> el<257:58>
